# The preferred citation is the dataset and its version on Hugging Face, so that GitHub's "Cite
# this repository" gives the dataset. Cite the source too, as FEMA asks (the dataset card's "How
# to cite"). Zenodo archives each GitHub release of this repository (the code and documents, no
# data) from .zenodo.json, which it reads in place of this file; the DOI below stands for all
# versions of the software and documents and resolves to the latest.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use WarnText, please cite the dataset and its version as below, and the source as FEMA asks (see the dataset card's How to cite)."
type: software
title: "WarnText: software and documentation for a research corpus of US public alert messages"
version: "0.2.0"
date-released: "2026-11-26"
abstract: >-
  WarnText is a research corpus of the public alert messages that US federal, state, local,
  tribal and territorial alerting authorities sent through the Integrated Public Alert and Warning
  System (IPAWS), read from FEMA's OpenFEMA dataset "IPAWS Archived Alerts" (version 1). Version
  0.2.0 holds every message in that archive from senders other than the National Weather
  Service, sent before 12:00 UTC on 25 November 2026: 34,876 real alerts from 1,260 alerting
  authorities and 49,310 tests and demonstrations, one row per message, with its alerting
  authority, its time, type and event codes, its places, the channels it asked for, the updates
  and cancellations that followed it, and measures of its text. The message text is included
  for the alerts that are not about people; the text of 13,934 real alerts is withheld (alerts about people by their event codes,
  texts about a missing person or that describe a person, and texts with a phone number or an
  email address), and their rows stay. It is a research record, not an alerting service. This
  repository holds the software that builds and checks the corpus, and its documents.
